{"dir":"out","ms":4565,"line":{"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":4,"method":"session/prompt","params":{"sessionId":"536c657b-d431-401a-a737-9b02c80e2240","prompt":[{"type":"text","text":"The workspace is `{{CWD}}`, not the directory you start in: name its files by their full path there.\n\nThis task is read-only: your own file edits, shell and web tools are off. Read files, search the repository and run tests with `run_command`, read web pages with `fetch` and search the web with `web_search`.\n\nWhen you have fully completed this task, call the `task_complete` tool with status `done` and your handoff. If you cannot complete it, call `task_complete` with status `blocked` or `failed` and the reason instead. Call it once, as your last action.\n\nIts `summary` is this task's handoff to the next task: the planner and the tasks that depend on this one read it first, and nothing else you write reaches them unless they ask. Write it for that reader, not as a log: what changed, what is left, what to watch. It is at most 1500 characters, and a call over a limit is refused: the long form goes in `report`.\n\nPut anything the next reader must act on in `findings`, one item each, most important first, each with a severity, a one-line title, its detail and evidence to check it by: a file (and line), a commit, a task (and one of its findings, as \"F3\") or a URL. Ordewell numbers them F1, F2, … so a later task can cite one. List what you produced in `artifacts`.\n\nThe long story, if there is one, goes in `report`: it is kept with this attempt and read only on demand. A review or other read-only task especially: its findings are its result, so state each one, and do not write the report to a file.\n\nThis is a test of your own tools. First use your own AskQuestion tool exactly once, to ask me whether to pick red or blue. Then use your own CreatePlan tool exactly once, to propose a one-step plan that picks a colour. Then complete the task through Ordewell with status \"done\" and a one-line summary of what each of those two tools answered, or that you have no such tool."}]}}}
